    <h1>1. Knots and their diagrams</h1>
    <p>Tie a knot in a piece of string and glue the two ends together. The result is a closed loop that cannot be untangled without cutting it, or so it seems. Knot theory turns “it seems” into mathematics. It asks when two closed loops in space are the same knot, and it answers by finding quantities, called invariants, that can be computed from a drawing and that do not change however the loop is moved. This chapter sets up the objects precisely, reduces the problem from three dimensions to drawings on paper, and then proves, with a short and complete argument, that the trefoil really is knotted.</p>

    <h2 id="knots">1.1 What a knot is</h2>
    <p>A <strong>knot</strong> is a closed curve in space that does not cross itself. Precisely, it is a subset $K \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ that is the image of a continuous injective map $S^1 \to \mathbb{R}^3$ from the circle. The <strong>unknot</strong> is the round circle $\{x^2 + y^2 = 1,\ z = 0\}$, and anything equivalent to it.</p>
    <p>As a topological space every knot is a circle. The map $S^1 \to K$ is a homeomorphism, so asking whether $K$ is homeomorphic to the unknot is asking nothing. What distinguishes knots is how the circle sits inside $\mathbb{R}^3$. The right notion of “the same” has to move the surrounding space along with the curve.</p>
    <p>Two knots $K_0$ and $K_1$ are <strong>equivalent</strong> when there is a continuous family of homeomorphisms $h_t: \mathbb{R}^3 \to \mathbb{R}^3$, $t \in [0, 1]$, with</p>
    $$
    h_0 = \mathrm{id}, \qquad h_1(K_0) = K_1.
    $$
    <p>Such a family is an <strong>ambient isotopy</strong>. Each $h_t$ deforms all of space, and the knot is carried along. Since $h_t$ is a bijection for every $t$, the curve can never pass through itself: two points of the curve that are distinct stay distinct.</p>
    <p>A weaker notion would be to deform the curve alone, through injective maps $S^1 \to \mathbb{R}^3$. That notion is useless, because of a trick sometimes called pulling the knot tight. Take a trefoil and shrink the knotted part to a smaller and smaller region, keeping the rest of the curve fixed. At every stage before the end, the curve is injective. At the end the knotted part has shrunk to a single point, and what remains is a circle. Every knot is deformable to the unknot this way. Ambient isotopy forbids it: a homeomorphism of space cannot squash a region containing a tangle into a point, because the tangle's complement would change.</p>
    <p>A second pathology has to be excluded by hand. A curve can be knotted infinitely often, with an infinite sequence of smaller and smaller trefoils converging to a point. Such <strong>wild</strong> knots exist and behave very differently. A knot is <strong>tame</strong> when it is equivalent to a polygon with finitely many edges, or equivalently to a smooth curve with nowhere-vanishing derivative. All knots from here on are tame. A <strong>link</strong> is the same definition with several disjoint circles, called its components. A knot is a link with one component.</p>
    <p>Whether the curve lives in $\mathbb{R}^3$ or in the three-sphere $S^3 = \mathbb{R}^3 \cup \{\infty\}$ makes no difference to the theory. A tame knot misses some point, and the equivalence classes are the same. The sphere is the more natural home later, when the complement $S^3 \setminus K$ becomes the main object of study.</p>
